\item \textbf{Empirical dissociation of evaluation metrics.}
$\rho$ up to 0.45 (explaining ${\sim}$20\% of variance) coexists with selection $\Delta{\approx}0$ against 13--18~pp oracle headroom. For instance-level signals, MBR consensus-proximity utility correlates with F1 across but not within instances; verbalized confidence achieves higher $\rho$ than LP yet the worst selection (Appendix~\ref{app:verbalized_confidence}). Single-metric evaluation risks selecting the wrong signal.

\item \textbf{LP compression and MBR failure.}
Log-probability, the only per-sample signal, is too compressed within instances to select (median range 0.041 nats, 60\% tied); consensus-proximity selection (MBR) correlates with F1 across instances but not within them. A 4-seed free-form ablation confirms compression is not caused by constrained decoding.

tool_args.new_text:
\item \textbf{The correlation-selection gap.}
$\rho$ up to 0.45 coexists with selection $\Delta{\approx}0$ against 13--18~pp oracle headroom. Log-probability---the only per-sample signal---is too compressed within instances to discriminate (median range 0.041 nats, pooled within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){=}0.248$); instance-level signals, even when converted to per-sample consensus-proximity rankings (MBR), do not improve selection because utility correlates with F1 across instances but not within them. Single-metric evaluation risks selecting the wrong signal.

\item \textbf{Diagnostic ablations.}
A 4-seed free-form decoding ablation confirms NER compression is not caused by constrained decoding; entity-token LP increases within-instance variance ($4{\times}$) but worsens selection, pointing to intrinsic model overconfidence on entity tokens. Verbalized confidence is even more compressed, confirming compression is not LP-specific (Appendix~\ref{app:verbalized_confidence}).

